    "narrative": "The board for May 5th points to **Bryson Stott** (72.63) and **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (72.31) as our primary drivers today. **Bryson Stott** benefits immensely from a favorable matchup against Severino, carrying a robust 14% skill edge against the SP while benefiting from a wind-aided environment that pushes his score well into the \"Strong\" tier. Similarly, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains a staple in our lineup; despite facing an elite hold candidate, his high base efficiency and prime batting order slot ensure he remains a reliable anchor for counting stats. **Matt Chapman** and **Jung Hoo Lee** are also elite plays, both exceeding the 68-point threshold thanks to a favorable matchup against Buehler; specifically, the combination of a slow delivery from the opposing starter\u2014which provides a stolen base boost\u2014and wind blowing out creates a high-ceiling environment for both players.\n\nRegarding bench decisions, **Gavin Sheets** was our closest consideration for the final UTIL spot, ultimately scoring a 45.22. While he held a marginal platoon advantage, his projection was suppressed by the tough matchup against Webb, who remains a high-floor arm capable of limiting total efficiency. Consequently, the optimizer favored the higher volatility and upside of our starters, particularly those facing the softer Buehler rotation. We are monitoring **Cal Raleigh** closely; while his score of 58.64 is respectable, his dynamic platoon splits indicate potential drag, and we are keeping a close eye on the official lineups to ensure his position in the two-hole holds. With **Yainer Diaz** still sidelined on the IL, we continue to rely on the depth of **Will Smith** to bridge the gap at catcher. Stay focused on the SB category today\u2014with the slow delivery of the pitchers facing **Matt Chapman**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, and **Daulton Varsho**, we have a tactical opportunity to gain ground in the scarcest category on the board.",
